1. Kubernetes Autoscaling Overview
    - Kubernetes autoscaling helps applications and infrastructure automatically adjust capacity based on load, traffic, or resource usage.
    - There are four major autoscaling mechanisms commonly used together:
    Autoscaler	        Scales What?	Based On	                Typical Use
    HPA	                Pods	        CPU, memory, custom metrics	Web/API traffic spikes
    VPA	                Pod resources	Historical usage	        Optimize CPU/RAM requests
    Cluster Autoscaler	Nodes	        Pending pods	            Add/remove worker nodes
    KEDA	            Pods	        External event sources	    Queue/event-driven workloads

2. HPA — Horizontal Pod Autoscaler
    - What it does
        HPA increases or decreases the number of pod replicas.
        - Example:
            Low traffic → 2 pods
            High traffic → 10 pods
        - It scales horizontally.
    - How HPA Works
        HPA continuously monitors metrics like:
            - CPU utilization
            - Memory utilization
            - Custom metrics
            - Prometheus metrics
        If utilization exceeds the threshold, Kubernetes creates more pods.
        - Example logic:
            Current CPU = 85%
            Target CPU = 50%
    - HPA increases replicas
    - Architecture
        Users → Service → Deployment → Pods
                                ↑
                                HPA
    - HPA talks to:
        - Metrics Server
        - Prometheus Adapter
        - Custom Metrics API
    - Example HPA Configuration
        apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
        kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
        metadata:
            name: api-hpa
        spec:
            scaleTargetRef:
                apiVersion: apps/v1
                kind: Deployment
                name: api-deployment
        minReplicas: 2
        maxReplicas: 10
        metrics:
        - type: Resource
            resource:
                name: cpu
                target:
                    type: Utilization
                    averageUtilization: 50
    - Visualization
        - CPU utilization target:
        Desired Replicas=⌈Current Replicas × Desired Metric Value/Current Metric Value⌉
    - Best Use Cases
        APIs
        Web applications
        Microservices
        Stateless applications
    - Pros
        Fast scaling
        Easy setup
        Native Kubernetes feature
    - Cons
        Requires good metrics
        Cold starts possible
        Not ideal for event-driven systems

3. VPA — Vertical Pod Autoscaler
    - What it does
        VPA changes the CPU and memory allocation of pods instead of changing pod count.
        It scales vertically.
    - Example:
        - Before:
        CPU: 200m
        Memory: 256Mi
        - After:
        CPU: 1000m
        Memory: 1Gi
        How VPA Works
    - VPA monitors historical resource usage and recommends or applies better values.
    - It can:
        Recommend resources
        Automatically update pod specs
        Restart pods with new resources
    - Architecture
        Pods → Usage Metrics → VPA Recommender
                                ↓
                        Update Resources
    - Example VPA Configuration
        apiVersion: autoscaling.k8s.io/v1
        kind: VerticalPodAutoscaler
        metadata:
            name: api-vpa
        spec:
            targetRef:
                apiVersion: apps/v1
                kind: Deployment
                name: api-deployment
            updatePolicy:
                updateMode: Auto
    - Modes
        Mode	    Behavior
        Off	        Only recommendations
        Initial	    Set resources at pod creation
        Auto	    Automatically update resources
    - Best Use Cases
        Stateful apps
        Databases
        JVM applications
        Memory-heavy workloads
    - Pros
        Better resource efficiency
        Reduces overprovisioning
        Good for unpredictable workloads
    - Cons
        Pod restarts required
        Can conflict with HPA
        Not ideal for latency-sensitive systems

4. Cluster Autoscaler
    - What it does
    Cluster Autoscaler adds or removes Kubernetes worker nodes.
    It scales the infrastructure layer.
    - Why it's needed
    Suppose HPA creates more pods:
        10 new pods created
        BUT
        No node has enough CPU/RAM
    Pods become:
        Pending
    Cluster Autoscaler detects this and creates new nodes.
    Architecture
        HPA → More Pods
                ↓
        Pending Pods
                ↓
        Cluster Autoscaler
                ↓
        Cloud Provider Creates Nodes
    - How It Works
    - Scale Up
        - When pods cannot be scheduled:
            Pending Pods → Add Nodes
    - Scale Down
        - When nodes stay underutilized:
            Unused Nodes → Remove Nodes
    - Supported Cloud Providers
        Amazon Web Services (EKS)
        Google Cloud (GKE)
        Microsoft (AKS)
    - Example Scenario
        Node Capacity = 4 CPUs
        Pods need 10 CPUs total
        → Autoscaler adds more nodes
    - Best Use Cases
        Dynamic workloads
        Cost optimization
        Cloud-native systems
    - Pros
        Reduces cloud costs
        Automatic infrastructure scaling
        Works with HPA
    - Cons
        Node startup delay
        Cloud-provider dependent
        Complex tuning sometimes

5. KEDA — Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling
    - What it does
        KEDA scales workloads based on external events instead of CPU/memory.
    - Examples:
        Kafka queue length
        RabbitMQ messages
        Redis streams
        Azure Service Bus
        AWS SQS
        Prometheus metrics
    - Why KEDA Exists
        HPA mainly works on:
            CPU
            Memory
        But event-driven systems need scaling based on:
            Queue size
            Event count
            Lag
            Message throughput
        KEDA solves this.
    - Architecture
        Kafka/RabbitMQ/SQS
                ↓
            KEDA
                ↓
            HPA
                ↓
            Pods
        - KEDA actually creates and manages HPA internally.
    - Example
        Suppose: RabbitMQ Queue = 10,000 messages
        KEDA scales consumers: 2 pods → 50 pods
    
    - Example KEDA Configuration
        apiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1
        kind: ScaledObject
        metadata:
            name: worker-scaledobject
        spec:
            scaleTargetRef:
                name: worker-deployment
            minReplicaCount: 0
            maxReplicaCount: 50
        triggers:
        - type: rabbitmq
            metadata:
                queueName: orders
                host: RabbitMQConnection
                queueLength: "100"
    - Special Feature: Scale to Zero
        KEDA can scale workloads to: 0 pods
        This saves cost for idle workloads.
    - Best Use Cases
        Background workers
        Event-driven systems
        Queue consumers
        Streaming systems
    - Pros
        Event-based scaling
        Scale-to-zero
        Large trigger ecosystem
    - Cons
        Additional component to manage
        More operational complexity

6. How They Work Together
    In real production systems:
        KEDA/HPA scales Pods
                ↓
        Pods exceed node capacity
                ↓
        Cluster Autoscaler adds Nodes
                ↓
        VPA optimizes resource allocation
    
7. Comparison Summary
    Feature	                HPA	        VPA	            Cluster Autoscaler	KEDA
    Scales Pods Count	    Yes	        No	            No	                Yes
    Scales CPU/RAM per Pod	No	        Yes	            No	                No
    Scales Nodes	        No	        No	            Yes	                No
    Uses CPU/Memory Metrics	Yes	        Yes	            Indirectly	        Optional
    Uses External Events	Limited	    No	            No	                Yes
    Scale to Zero	        No	        No	            No	                Yes
    Best For	            APIs	    Stateful apps	Infrastructure	    Event-driven apps